PLANETARY INVISIBLE ENVIRONMENT PERCEPTION

A Physics-Constrained Multimodal Architecture for Reconstructing Invisible Environmental States on Earth, Mars, and the Moon

---

Research Draft v0.4 — September 2026

Research Coordinator: Ouadi Maakoul

Research Program: For Humanity 4

Status: Foundational research draft — literature-grounded, mathematically formalized, experimentally scoped, open to independent review and falsification

AI: ChatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI

Abstract

Human perception is biologically constrained to a narrow band of the electromagnetic spectrum and a limited set of direct physical sensations. A vast array of physical variables that determine environmental safety, habitability, navigability, and scientific value — atmospheric pressure fields, three-dimensional temperature structure, humidity gradients, wind vectors, refractive-index variations, radiation topologies, and dust concentrations — remain perceptually invisible without technological mediation. The central hypothesis of this research is not that a new sensor must be invented to render these phenomena perceptible. Individual sensing modalities already exist in mature form: weather radar can retrieve atmospheric refractivity from phase measurements of stationary targets; differential-absorption radar can retrieve surface air pressure through oxygen absorption lines; hyperspectral microwave radiometers can retrieve temperature and humidity profiles with high information content; Doppler wind lidar can derive virtual potential temperature from turbulent kinetic energy budget residuals; and planetary missions have deployed comprehensive environmental sensor suites on Mars and the Moon. The scientific opportunity is architectural: to fuse these heterogeneous, partial observations into a continuously updated, physics-constrained, uncertainty-quantified four-dimensional representation of environmental states that humans and autonomous systems can perceive and act upon.

We call this framework Planetary Invisible Environment Perception (PIEP) . PIEP treats the environment as a latent physical state X(\mathbf{x}, t) = [P, T, q, \mathbf{u}, N, D, R, \Phi, \ldots] rather than as a collection of isolated sensor measurements. This version (v0.4) incorporates major revisions: (i) a formal Related Architectures section positioning PIEP against operational digital-twin programs including ECMWF's Destination Earth and NASA's Earth System Digital Twins; (ii) a patent and prior-art analysis bounding the novelty claim to latent-space multimodal fusion rather than differential-absorption radar itself; (iii) a corrected Bayesian identifiability framework using the posterior precision matrix rather than the data-only observability Gramian; (iv) a redesigned OSSE protocol using a nature run with distinct high-fidelity and low-fidelity observation operators; (v) Mars-specific assimilation failure modes including thermal-tide bias, dust assimilation limitations, and MEDA sensor-loss modeling; (vi) a corrected lunar differential brightness temperature method with proper Chang'E microwave radiometer frequencies; (vii) computational-latency falsifiability criteria; and (viii) a conditional value-at-risk decision formalism for the human/machine interface. Falsifiability criteria are now tied to operational thresholds with explicit units. The framework is presented as a foundational research program supporting human habitation, autonomous robotics, navigation, infrastructure management, and scientific discovery across Earth, Mars, and the Moon.

