2. From "Seeing the Invisible" to Environmental State Reconstruction

The initial intuition behind this research can be expressed simply: if humans cannot directly see a physical phenomenon, can technology transform its measurable physical signature into a perceptible representation?

The answer is demonstrated across multiple scientific domains. A radar does not "see" humidity directly. However, propagation through the atmosphere modifies electromagnetic signals, and these changes contain information about atmospheric refractivity. Weather radars can retrieve refractivity changes based on phase variations of stationary targets, providing information on moisture in the atmospheric boundary layer. These retrievals have been shown to correlate with in-situ measurements from automatic weather stations, though with documented limitations in vertical resolution and aliasing susceptibility.

A microwave radiometer does not photograph temperature layers. It measures microwave radiance whose spectral dependence encodes information about atmospheric temperature and water vapor. Hyperspectral microwave radiometers with thousands of channels around the 118 GHz oxygen absorption complex can jointly retrieve atmospheric temperature and humidity profiles under clear-sky conditions, with information content that substantially exceeds conventional operational sounders.

A differential-absorption radar does not visually observe atmospheric pressure. It exploits frequency-dependent absorption, particularly oxygen absorption, to infer atmospheric attenuation and ultimately surface pressure when combined with ancillary atmospheric information. NASA's Microwave Barometric Radar and Sounder (MBARS) measures the surface attenuated radar cross section (ARCS) at three sets of frequencies along the oxygen absorption line, enabling estimation of atmospheric attenuation due to oxygen while suppressing the impact of surface normalized cross section, water vapor, and clouds. MBARS has now flown on the NASA ER-2 high-altitude aircraft during summer and fall 2024, demonstrating preliminary sea level pressure retrievals consistent with science requirements, with the caveat that the analysis remains under open peer review.

These examples demonstrate a general principle:

\text{Physical phenomenon} \rightarrow \text{measurable signature} \rightarrow \text{inverse problem} \rightarrow \text{estimated state} \rightarrow \text{human/robot perception}.

The proposed contribution is to treat this chain as one integrated, generalizable environmental perception architecture.

