3. State of the Art

3.1 Radar Refractivity and Its Identifiability Limits

Weather radar can exploit phase variations in echoes from stationary ground targets. If a target is stationary, changes in measured phase can be associated with changes in the atmospheric refractive index along the radar path. Atmospheric refractivity depends on pressure, temperature, and water vapor. The technique was initially developed by Fabry et al. (1997) and has since been applied to operational weather radars at C-band. Maps of this parameter have been established in several measurement campaigns, demonstrating the possibility of retrieving refractivity within a radius of 30 km around the radar with a temporal resolution of 15 minutes.

However, this technique has fundamental limitations that must be explicitly acknowledged in any architecture that incorporates it. Radar refractivity does not uniquely determine pressure, temperature, and humidity. The refractivity N is given by:

N \approx 77.6\frac{P}{T} + 6.04\times 10^5\frac{Pq}{T^2}

where P is pressure, T is temperature, and q is specific humidity. A single refractivity observation cannot uniquely determine all three variables. The effects of humidity and temperature are not separable from refractivity measurements alone. Additional failure modes include phase aliasing problems due to temporal and spatial undersampling, which increase with radar frequency and integration range. Comparisons with in-situ measurements have shown significant bias and large variance due to limitations in estimating the vertical refractivity gradient.

Therefore, the architecture must treat N as an observation constraint on the atmospheric state, not as a direct measurement of any single thermodynamic variable.

3.2 Hyperspectral Microwave Atmospheric Profiling

Microwave radiometers provide a complementary observation modality with rapidly advancing capabilities. Multi-channel microwave measurements around water-vapor and oxygen absorption features contain information about atmospheric temperature and humidity profiles. Because microwave signals penetrate clouds more effectively than infrared observations, microwave techniques provide valuable complementary information under cloudy conditions.

Recent advances in hyperspectral microwave instrumentation have substantially increased the information content available for atmospheric sounding. A 2025–2026 information content study for the Bureau of Meteorology microwave-sounding mission (MSM) computed degrees of freedom for signal (DFS) for temperature and water vapor using the column representation of the Bureau's operational background error covariance matrix. The results show that information content is highest when the instrument is configured to a 10-MHz bandwidth in the 50–60-GHz band, with single-sideband 20-MHz and 40-MHz bandwidths in the 118- and 183-GHz bands, respectively. The study indicates that the hyperspectral MSM would provide considerably increased information content relative to the Advanced Technology Microwave Sounder (ATMS) and other existing operational microwave sounders.

This has direct architectural implications. The microwave forward model H_{\mathrm{MW}}(X) should not be treated as a low-information constraint. With hyperspectral configurations, the degrees of freedom for signal can exceed 10 for temperature, meaning that the instrument provides genuine vertical resolution rather than a single-column average. The PIEP inversion must therefore treat the microwave observation as a high-dimensional constraint with a properly characterized observation error covariance R, not as a scalar measurement.

Nevertheless, microwave retrievals remain ill-posed and require prior information. The retrieved profile should not be interpreted as an infinitely detailed direct measurement. The system must estimate not only the state \hat{X} but also its uncertainty \Sigma_X.

3.3 Differential-Absorption Radar and Surface Pressure: Flown Results

NASA's Microwave Barometric Radar and Sounder (MBARS) provides the strongest Earth-based experimental evidence that an otherwise invisible atmospheric variable — surface pressure — can be remotely sensed with operational precision. MBARS is a combined active and passive microwave sensor that measures surface air pressure in the oxygen absorption V-band (64–70 GHz). It includes a cross-track scanning differential absorption radar (DAR) to estimate total atmospheric oxygen content and determine surface dry air pressure. The instrument operates with channels around 65.5, 67.75, and 70 GHz on the upper side of the V-band oxygen absorption feature.

MBARS flew for the first time on the NASA ER-2 high-altitude aircraft during summer and fall 2024 for test flights and the Westcoast & Heartland Hyperspectral Microwave Sensor Intensive Experiment (WH2yMSIE) field campaign. During these flights, it collected ARCS data over a variety of atmospheric conditions. Data from recent flights show preliminary sea level pressure retrievals consistent with science requirements, with a target precision of 1–2 hPa surface pressure. The design standard error is approximately 0.2% of surface pressure, equivalent to about 2 hPa at standard sea level conditions.

Important caveat. The MBARS manuscript is currently an open preprint under discussion for Atmospheric Measurement Techniques, submitted March 2026 with discussion opened in July 2026. The one referee report on record requests additional analysis of how integration time, footprint size, and pressure-gradient regime affect the precision claims. The PIEP architecture must treat the MBARS precision figure as a strong, credible preliminary result rather than a settled operational specification. PIEP's own Layer 2 error-budget propagation will inherit whatever constraints the peer-review process eventually imposes on that claim.

The MBARS error budget includes several components that must be propagated through the PIEP architecture: ARCS calibration uncertainty, surface normalized radar cross section variability, water vapor and cloud correction residuals, and the ancillary temperature and water vapor profile uncertainties required to convert oxygen column absorption to dry air pressure. This instrument demonstrates that the original intuition — remotely sensing an otherwise invisible atmospheric pressure field — is physically meaningful and experimentally promising. It also demonstrates why the proposed architecture should not be framed as a single "magic radar." The MBARS retrieval requires ancillary temperature and water vapor information. The future system is more plausibly a multimodal inverse-physics platform in which pressure is one component of a jointly estimated state vector.

3.4 Doppler Lidar for Wind and Thermodynamic Profiling

Doppler wind lidar provides a critical observation modality that has recently expanded beyond wind measurement alone. A 2026 study presents a Doppler wind LiDAR–based retrieval that derives virtual potential temperature from turbulent kinetic energy (TKE) budget buoyancy residuals, enabling concurrent profiling of wind, turbulence, and thermal structure from a single instrument. Field applications in Shenzhen and Beijing demonstrate that the method resolves stable, convective, and low-level-jet layers, capturing turbulence–stability interactions with sub-kelvin accuracy. These findings establish that conventional wind LiDAR can be transformed into a continuous, high-resolution dynamic–thermodynamic profiler for detailed investigation of boundary-layer processes.

For PIEP, this modality is particularly valuable because it provides a thermodynamic constraint — virtual potential temperature — that is independent of microwave radiometer retrievals and radar refractivity. The observation operator H_{\mathrm{lidar}}(X) must therefore map the state vector to both wind and virtual potential temperature signatures.

3.5 GNSS Radio Occultation

GNSS radio occultation (RO) provides atmospheric profiles with independent information on altitude and pressure. The observational uncertainty of individual temperature/pressure/potential temperature profiles is approximately 0.7 K / 0.15% / 1.4 K in the tropopause region. The total climatological error of mean atmospheric fields is generally dominated by the systematic error component. GNSS-RO provides a globally distributed, absolutely calibrated pressure anchor that is independent of surface-based instruments and microwave radiance measurements. In the PIEP architecture, this modality serves as a critical constraint for breaking degeneracies between pressure, temperature, and humidity in the lower troposphere.

3.6 Data Assimilation and Latent-Space Machine Learning

Modern atmospheric data assimilation increasingly incorporates machine learning. The classical variational data assimilation cost function is:

J(x)=\frac{1}{2}(x-x_b)^T B^{-1}(x-x_b)+\frac{1}{2}[y-H(x)]^T R^{-1}[y-H(x)]

where x_b is the background field, B is the background error covariance, R is the observation error covariance, H is the observation operator, and y is the observation vector. For 4DVar, the cost function incorporates model dynamics:

J(x)=\frac{1}{2}(x-x_b)^T B^{-1}(x-x_b)+\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=0}^{n}[y_i-H(M_{0\rightarrow i}(x))]^T R_i^{-1}[y_i-H(M_{0\rightarrow i}(x))]

A 2025–2026 breakthrough reformulates model training as a four-dimensional variational data assimilation problem that treats reanalysis data as imperfect observations. This enables the loss function to incorporate cross-variable error covariance structures that capture multivariate dependencies and their associated errors. In practice, this objective is approximated by computing the loss in an autoencoder-learned latent space of global atmospheric states. By encoding complex nonlinear couplings among atmospheric variables, this representation allows the high-dimensional, complex error covariance matrix in model space to be approximated as nearly diagonal in latent space, substantially simplifying implementation. Rollout training with latent-space constraints improves long-term forecast skill while better preserving fine-scale structures and physical realism than model-space loss. The framework extends to accommodate heterogeneous data sources, enabling the forecast model to be trained jointly on reanalysis and multi-source observations within a unified theoretical formulation.

A distinct but closely related paper by largely the same group performs Bayesian data assimilation directly in an autoencoder latent space, reporting that latent-space assimilation improves both analysis quality and forecast skill relative to model-space DA. These two papers serve different roles in the PIEP architecture:

· arXiv:2510.04006 (Fan et al., 2025) — how the background model M and its training loss are formulated.
· arXiv:2502.02884 (Fan et al., 2025) — justification for performing the inference-time assimilation in latent space z = \mathrm{Encoder}(X).

This distinction matters because the PIEP cost function J(z,\theta) is an inference-time object, not a training-time loss. The two references are not interchangeable, and the architecture should cite them for what they actually support.

This latent-space formulation provides the architectural blueprint for PIEP's Layer 4. The background model M can be an ML emulator trained on physical reanalysis data, and the data assimilation can be performed in latent space z = \mathrm{Encoder}(X), with decoding to the physical environmental state \mathcal{E}. This addresses the research question of how physics constraints can be enforced, though—as Section 5.5.1 clarifies—"physical consistency by construction" must be demonstrated, not merely asserted.

