5. Planetary Invisible Environment Perception (PIEP)

We propose the following conceptual architecture, organized as a nine-stage closed estimation loop:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Sensors} \rightarrow \text{Calibration} \rightarrow \text{Cross-Calibration} \rightarrow \text{Forward Models} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Multimodal Inversion} \rightarrow \text{Data Assimilation} \rightarrow \text{Uncertainty Calibration} \\
&\rightarrow \text{4D State} \rightarrow \text{Human/Robotic Perception} \rightarrow \text{Decision Interface}
\end{aligned}
}

Note: the previous version labeled this an "eight-layer" loop while defining nine stages and omitting Layer 6 from the diagram. This version corrects both inconsistencies.

5.1 Layer 1 — Physical Sensing

The observation set is:

Y = \{ Y_{\mathrm{radar}}, Y_{\mathrm{lidar}}, Y_{\mathrm{MW}}, Y_{\mathrm{IR}}, Y_{\mathrm{GNSS}}, Y_{\mathrm{surface}}, Y_{\mathrm{orbital}}, Y_{\mathrm{pol}} \}

where Y_{\mathrm{lidar}} denotes Doppler wind lidar measurements (wind and TKE-budget-derived virtual potential temperature) and Y_{\mathrm{surface}} denotes in-situ surface packages (e.g., MEDA-class instrumentation on Mars, surface stations on Earth). Y_{\mathrm{orbital}} denotes orbital sounding and imaging (e.g., MCS, Diviner, Chang'E MRM). Y_{\mathrm{pol}} denotes polarimetric measurements for aerosol and dust characterization.

Not every planet requires every sensor. The sensor suite is selected according to the dominant invisible environmental variables for each planetary regime.

5.2 Layer 2 — Sensor Calibration and Quality Control

Each observation is associated with a calibration state vector \theta_i, timestamp, geometry, spatial resolution, temporal resolution, signal-to-noise characteristics, environmental contamination flags, uncertainty, and provenance metadata. This layer is essential because multimodal fusion can otherwise create false confidence: multiple poorly calibrated sensors agreeing on an incorrect value produce a high-confidence erroneous state estimate.

5.3 Layer 2.5 — Inter-Sensor Cross-Calibration

This layer addresses systematic biases between sensors that observe overlapping state variables. For example, radar refractivity retrievals require selection of a reference state, and different reference-state choices produce different refractivity maps. Microwave radiometer temperature retrievals may have systematic offsets relative to GNSS-RO temperature profiles. Doppler lidar virtual potential temperature retrievals may exhibit biases relative to radiosonde measurements. Cross-calibration identifies and corrects these biases before they propagate into the fused state estimate.

5.4 Layer 3 — Forward Physical Models

Each sensor is represented by an observation operator:

Y_i = H_i(X, \theta_i) + \epsilon_i

where X is the environmental state, \theta_i represents sensor calibration parameters, H_i is the physical forward model associated with sensor i, and \epsilon_i represents measurement and model error, decomposed as:

\epsilon_i = \epsilon_{\mathrm{instr}} + \epsilon_{\mathrm{repr}} + \epsilon_{H}

with \epsilon_{\mathrm{instr}} the instrumental noise, \epsilon_{\mathrm{repr}} the representativeness error arising from unresolved scales, and \epsilon_{H} the forward-model error.

The observation operators are aligned with the observation set in Section 5.1 as follows:

Observation Forward model Primary state variables
Y_{\mathrm{radar}} H_{\mathrm{radar}}(X,\theta) P, T, q via N
Y_{\mathrm{lidar}} H_{\mathrm{lidar}}(X,\theta) \mathbf{u}, \theta_v
Y_{\mathrm{MW}} H_{\mathrm{MW}}(X,\theta) T, q profiles
Y_{\mathrm{IR}} H_{\mathrm{IR}}(X,\theta) T, surface properties
Y_{\mathrm{GNSS}} H_{\mathrm{RO}}(X,\theta) P, T via bending angle
Y_{\mathrm{surface}} H_{\mathrm{surf}}(X,\theta) P, T, q, \mathbf{u}, D, R
Y_{\mathrm{orbital}} H_{\mathrm{orb}}(X,\theta) T, P, q, D profiles
Y_{\mathrm{pol}} H_{\mathrm{pol}}(D,\theta) D, particle size/shape

This formulation explicitly treats calibration parameters as unknowns to be estimated jointly with the state, rather than assumed known.

5.5 Layer 4 — Multimodal Inversion and Data Assimilation

The central mathematical problem is expressed as a Bayesian hierarchical inference:

p(X, \theta \mid Y) \propto p(Y \mid X, \theta) \, p(X \mid \theta) \, p(\theta)

The maximum a posteriori estimate is obtained by minimizing the negative log-posterior:

\hat{X}_t, \hat{\theta} = \arg\min_{X, \theta} \left[ \frac{1}{2}\| X - X_b \|^2_{B^{-1}} + \frac{1}{2}\sum_i \| Y_i - H_i(X, \theta_i) \|^2_{R_i^{-1}} + \lambda R(X) \right]

subject to physical constraints C(X) = 0 and inequality constraints G(X) \leq 0.

In the latent-space formulation, the inversion is performed in a learned latent representation z = \mathrm{Encoder}(X):

J(z, \theta) = \frac{1}{2}\| z - z_b \|^2_{B_z^{-1}} + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=0}^{n}\| y_i - H_i(\mathrm{Decoder}(z), \theta_i) \|^2_{R_i^{-1}}

where B_z is the background error covariance in latent space, approximated as nearly diagonal due to the autoencoder's nonlinear coupling of physical variables.

5.5.1 Treatment of Physical Constraints in Latent Space

The v0.3 draft defined C(X)=0 and G(X)\leq 0 in state space but moved optimization into latent space without specifying how constraints propagate. This gap is closed here. Three enforcement mechanisms are possible, and PIEP uses a hybrid:

1. Architectural enforcement. The decoder \mathrm{Decoder}(z) is constructed so that its output automatically satisfies conservation laws (mass, momentum, energy) by construction. This is the strongest guarantee but requires a physics-informed decoder architecture. It is used for the conservation constraints C_{\mathrm{cons}}(X)=0.
2. Penalty on decoded state. A soft penalty \mu\|C(\mathrm{Decoder}(z))\|^2 is added to J(z,\theta). This is used for inequality constraints G(X)\leq 0 via a barrier or hinge function. The weight \mu is treated as a hyperparameter and calibrated on validation data.
3. Projection at each iteration. After each optimization step, the decoded state is projected onto the feasible set \Omega = \{X : C(X)=0, G(X)\leq 0\}. This is exact but computationally expensive and is reserved for constraints that cannot be enforced architecturally.

The claim "physical consistency by construction" from Section 3.6 is therefore a design target, not an automatic property. The Phase I OSSE must report which constraints are enforced by which mechanism and quantify the residual \|C(\hat{X})\| as a falsifiability metric.

5.6 Layer 4.5 — Uncertainty Calibration

The raw posterior uncertainty \Sigma_X from the inversion may be miscalibrated. This layer applies calibration techniques to ensure that the predicted uncertainty matches the observed error distribution. Metrics include:

· Continuous Ranked Probability Score (CRPS) against the nature run.
· Probability Integral Transform (PIT) histograms with a Kolmogorov–Smirnov statistic threshold.
· Spread-skill ratio \sigma_{\mathrm{spread}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{error}}, required to lie within a target interval.
· Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection. If the encoder encounters a state far from its training distribution, the system must flag low confidence rather than hallucinate a physically plausible but wrong field. OOD detection is implemented via latent-space Mahalanobis distance and reconstruction-error thresholds.

If calibration diagnostics exceed operational thresholds (Section 14), the uncertainty estimates are deemed unreliable and must be corrected before presentation to users.

5.7 Layer 5 — Four-Dimensional Environmental State

The final product is a four-dimensional representation \mathcal{E}(x, y, z, t) containing multiple physical layers:

\mathcal{E} = [P, T, q, \mathbf{u}, D, R, N, \Phi, \ldots]

5.8 Layer 6 — Human and Robotic Perception

The goal is not to replace human perception. It is to extend it. A human operator interacts with the environment as a dynamic physical field. Temperature becomes a visible thermal boundary structure. Pressure gradients become an evolving scalar field. Wind becomes a vector field. Humidity gradients become spatial structures. Dust concentration becomes a volumetric layer. Radiation becomes a spatial risk topology. Uncertainty becomes part of the environment itself: unknown regions are explicitly represented rather than hidden.

5.9 Layer 6.5 — Decision Interface

The state estimate is transformed into actionable information through a conditional value-at-risk policy:

\pi^*(X, U_X) = \arg\min_{a} \; \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha\left[ L(a, X) \mid \hat{X}, U_X \right]

where L(a, X) is a loss function for action a under true state X, and \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha is the conditional value-at-risk at confidence level \alpha (e.g., \alpha=0.95). This replaces the informal "SafeRegion" formulation with a risk-quantified decision rule.

For example, a rover planning a traverse would solve:

a^* = \arg\min_{a} \; \mathrm{CVaR}_{0.95}\left[ L_{\mathrm{thermal}}(a, T) + L_{\mathrm{power}}(a, D) \mid \hat{T}, \hat{D}, U_T, U_D \right]

This makes perception actionable and testable in Phase V human-factors experiments.

