6. Identifiability and Observability Analysis

6.1 Degeneracy Analysis

The refractivity equation demonstrates the fundamental degeneracy:

N \approx 77.6\frac{P}{T} + 6.04\times 10^5\frac{Pq}{T^2}

A single N observation has infinitely many (P, T, q) solutions. The Jacobian \partial N / \partial (P, T, q) has rank 1, so the null space is two-dimensional. Additional observations must break this degeneracy.

The corrected sensor-combination table is:

Sensor combination Variable separated Mechanism
N + MWR + surface P q MWR provides T profile; surface station provides P; radar provides N; solve for q
GNSS-RO + MWR P, T RO provides independent P and T with altitude; MWR provides complementary profile
\theta_v + MWR + surface P T, q Lidar provides \theta_v; MWR provides humidity or temperature constraint; surface P anchors the column
\theta_v + surface P, q T Lidar provides \theta_v; surface provides P and q; solve for T
N + GNSS-RO + surface P q profile RO breaks P–T degeneracy; radar constrains humidity

Correction from v0.3: the row "\theta_v + surface P → T,q" was underdetermined. One \theta_v observation with P known gives one equation for two unknowns. The corrected rows require an additional constraint.

6.2 Posterior Precision and Observability

For the linearized observation system \delta Y = H \delta X, the posterior precision matrix is:

\mathcal{P} = B^{-1} + \sum_i H_i^T R_i^{-1} H_i

The eigenvalues of \mathcal{P} quantify the information content for each state direction, including the prior constraint. Directions with small eigenvalues are poorly constrained by both data and prior. For nonlinear systems, the Jacobian H_i is evaluated at the MAP estimate, or the empirical observability Gramian is used.

Correction from v0.3: the data-only Gramian \mathcal{O} = \sum_i H_i^T \Sigma_i^{-1} H_i overstates which directions are unobservable by ignoring the prior. The posterior precision is the correct object for Bayesian inversion.

