7. The Importance of Uncertainty

A critical design principle of PIEP is: the system must never confuse visualization with knowledge. A beautiful environmental field can be scientifically wrong.

Therefore the system estimates the posterior distribution p(X \mid Y) rather than only producing a point estimate \hat{X}. The user-facing representation displays (\hat{X}, U_X) where U_X represents uncertainty. For example: T = 213.4 \pm 4.2 \;\mathrm{K} rather than simply T = 213.4 \;\mathrm{K}.

The system identifies regions as high confidence, moderate confidence, poorly constrained, extrapolated, sensor-conflicted, physically inconsistent, or out-of-distribution. Uncertainty calibration (Layer 4.5) ensures that the predicted uncertainty is statistically consistent with the observed error distribution.

