A.2 State Vector Definition

The environmental state at spatial location \mathbf{x} and time t is:

X(\mathbf{x}, t) = \big[\, P,\; T,\; q,\; \mathbf{u},\; N,\; D,\; R,\; \Phi,\; \ldots \,\big]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{n_X}

where P is pressure (Pa), T temperature (K), q specific humidity (kg kg⁻¹), \mathbf{u} = (u,v,w) wind vector (m s⁻¹), N radio refractivity (dimensionless), D dust/aerosol concentration (kg m⁻³), R radiation flux (W m⁻²), \Phi illumination or radiative state (W m⁻²).

For the lunar regime, the state vector is replaced by:

X_{\mathrm{Moon}}(\mathbf{x}, t) = \big[\, T,\; \Phi,\; S,\; R,\; D,\; G,\; I \,\big]^\top

with S shadow state, G terrain/geometry, I inferred infrastructure interaction.

A.3 Observation Space

The concatenated observation vector is:

Y = \big[\, Y_1^\top,\; Y_2^\top,\; \ldots,\; Y_M^\top \,\big]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{n_Y}, \quad n_Y = \sum_{i=1}^{M} n_{Y_i}

with block-diagonal nominal error covariance:

R = \mathrm{blkdiag}(R_1, R_2, \ldots, R_M)

Remark A.1. Block-diagonality of R is an approximation. Cross-sensor error correlations (e.g., shared representativeness error when two sensors resolve the same unresolved scales) violate it. Section A.4 addresses this.

A.4 Observation Error Decomposition

Each sensor's error is decomposed as:

\epsilon_i = \epsilon_i^{\mathrm{instr}} + \epsilon_i^{\mathrm{repr}} + \epsilon_i^{H}

where:

· \epsilon_i^{\mathrm{instr}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R_i^{\mathrm{instr}}): instrumental noise.
· \epsilon_i^{\mathrm{repr}}: representativeness error, arising from the mismatch between the point-like state X(\mathbf{x},t) and the sensor's spatial-temporal averaging kernel.
· \epsilon_i^{H}: forward-model error, arising from approximations in H_i.

Proposition A.1 (Representativeness covariance). Let W_i(\mathbf{x},t) be the sensor's spatial-temporal weighting kernel. Then:

\epsilon_i^{\mathrm{repr}} = \int W_i(\mathbf{x}',t')\, \big[ H_i(X(\mathbf{x}',t')) - H_i(\bar X_i) \big]\, d\mathbf{x}'\, dt'

where \bar X_i = \int W_i X\, d\mathbf{x}'\, dt' is the sensor-weighted mean. If X is a stationary random field with covariance C_X(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}'), then:

\mathrm{Cov}(\epsilon_i^{\mathrm{repr}}) = \int\!\!\int W_i(\mathbf{x}) W_i(\mathbf{x}') \, \nabla H_i\, C_X(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}')\, \nabla H_i^\top \, d\mathbf{x}\, d\mathbf{x}'

Proof. Direct application of the definition and Fubini's theorem. The linearization \nabla H_i is evaluated at \bar X_i. \square

Corollary A.1.1. The cross-sensor representativeness covariance between sensors i and j is:

\mathrm{Cov}(\epsilon_i^{\mathrm{repr}}, \epsilon_j^{\mathrm{repr}}) = \int\!\!\int W_i(\mathbf{x}) W_j(\mathbf{x}') \, \nabla H_i\, C_X(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}')\, \nabla H_j^\top \, d\mathbf{x}\, d\mathbf{x}'

This is nonzero whenever W_i and W_j overlap in space-time and C_X is not diagonal. This justifies treating R as a full (not block-diagonal) matrix in the PIEP inversion. The block-diagonal approximation is only valid when sensor footprints are disjoint or when C_X is delta-correlated.

