Appendix B — Bayesian Hierarchical Inference: Full Derivation

B.1 Joint Posterior

The full Bayesian model is:

p(X, \theta \mid Y) = \frac{p(Y \mid X, \theta)\, p(X \mid \theta)\, p(\theta)}{p(Y)}

with the marginal likelihood (evidence):

p(Y) = \int\!\!\int p(Y \mid X, \theta)\, p(X \mid \theta)\, p(\theta)\, dX\, d\theta

B.2 Likelihood

Assuming conditionally independent Gaussian observation errors given (X, \theta):

p(Y \mid X, \theta) = \prod_{i=1}^{M} (2\pi)^{-n_{Y_i}/2} |R_i|^{-1/2} \exp\!\left[ -\frac{1}{2} \| Y_i - H_i(X, \theta_i) \|_{R_i^{-1}}^2 \right]

Remark B.1. This factorization assumes conditional independence given the true state. Appendix A.4 shows that this is not exactly true when representativeness errors are correlated. In practice, we either (i) inflate R_i to absorb cross-correlations, or (ii) use a full R matrix in the inversion. PIEP adopts (ii) where the cross-covariance can be estimated from the nature run.

B.3 Prior on State

The state prior is Gaussian:

p(X \mid \theta) = (2\pi)^{-n_X/2} |B|^{-1/2} \exp\!\left[ -\frac{1}{2} \| X - X_b \|_{B^{-1}}^2 \right]

In the latent-space formulation, X = \mathrm{Dec}(z) and:

p(z) = (2\pi)^{-n_z/2} |B_z|^{-1/2} \exp\!\left[ -\frac{1}{2} \| z - z_b \|_{B_z^{-1}}^2 \right]

B.4 Prior on Calibration Parameters

p(\theta) = \mathcal{N}(\theta_b, B_\theta)

with B_\theta encoding prior knowledge of calibration uncertainties (e.g., from laboratory characterization of MBARS, MEDA pressure sensor, etc.).

B.5 MAP Estimation: Full Cost Function

Taking -\log p(X,\theta \mid Y) and dropping constants:

J(X, \theta) = \frac{1}{2} \| X - X_b \|_{B^{-1}}^2 + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i=1}^{M} \| Y_i - H_i(X, \theta_i) \|_{R_i^{-1}}^2 + \frac{1}{2} \| \theta - \theta_b \|_{B_\theta^{-1}}^2

subject to:

C(X) = 0, \quad G(X) \leq 0

Theorem B.1 (Gradient of the cost function). The gradient of J with respect to X is:

\nabla_X J = B^{-1}(X - X_b) - \sum_{i=1}^{M} K_i^\top R_i^{-1} \big[ Y_i - H_i(X, \theta_i) \big]

where K_i = \partial H_i / \partial X is the Jacobian of the forward model.

Proof. Direct differentiation. The gradient of the quadratic prior term is B^{-1}(X - X_b). The gradient of the likelihood term uses the chain rule: \partial/\partial X \| Y_i - H_i \|^2_{R_i^{-1}} = -2 K_i^\top R_i^{-1}(Y_i - H_i). \square

Corollary B.1.1 (Gradient with respect to \theta).

\nabla_\theta J = B_\theta^{-1}(\theta - \theta_b) - \sum_{i=1}^{M} L_i^\top R_i^{-1} \big[ Y_i - H_i(X, \theta_i) \big]

where L_i = \partial H_i / \partial \theta_i.

B.6 Latent-Space Cost Function

With X = \mathrm{Dec}(z):

J(z, \theta) = \frac{1}{2} \| z - z_b \|_{B_z^{-1}}^2 + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i=1}^{M} \| Y_i - H_i(\mathrm{Dec}(z), \theta_i) \|_{R_i^{-1}}^2 + \frac{1}{2} \| \theta - \theta_b \|_{B_\theta^{-1}}^2

Theorem B.2 (Latent-space gradient). The gradient with respect to z is:

\nabla_z J = B_z^{-1}(z - z_b) - \sum_{i=1}^{M} J_{\mathrm{Dec}}^\top K_i^\top R_i^{-1} \big[ Y_i - H_i(\mathrm{Dec}(z), \theta_i) \big]

where J_{\mathrm{Dec}} = \partial \mathrm{Dec}(z) / \partial z is the decoder Jacobian.

Proof. Chain rule: \nabla_z H_i(\mathrm{Dec}(z), \theta_i) = K_i J_{\mathrm{Dec}}. \square

Remark B.2 (Computational advantage). The dimension of z is n_z \ll n_X. The Hessian of J in latent space is n_z \times n_z, while in state space it is n_X \times n_X. For Earth reanalysis states with n_X \sim 10^8 and n_z \sim 10^3, this is a 10⁵ reduction in the dimension of the optimization problem.

