Appendix C — Identifiability Theory

C.1 Structural vs Practical Identifiability

Definition C.1 (Structural identifiability). The state X is structurally identifiable from observation Y under forward model H if the map H: X \mapsto Y is injective on the feasible set \Omega = \{X : C(X)=0, G(X)\leq 0\}.

Definition C.2 (Practical identifiability). The state X is practically identifiable from observation Y at noise level R if the posterior precision matrix \mathcal{P} = B^{-1} + H^\top R^{-1} H has no eigenvalue below a threshold \lambda_{\min} corresponding to operational requirements.

C.2 Rank Analysis of the Refractivity Operator

Theorem C.1 (Rank deficiency of refractivity). Let X = (P, T, q) and N(X) = 77.6 P/T + 6.04\times 10^5 P q/T^2. Then the Jacobian \partial N / \partial X has rank 1 everywhere on \Omega.

Proof. Compute the partial derivatives:

\frac{\partial N}{\partial P} = \frac{77.6}{T} + \frac{6.04\times 10^5 q}{T^2}

\frac{\partial N}{\partial T} = -\frac{77.6 P}{T^2} - \frac{1.208\times 10^6 P q}{T^3}

\frac{\partial N}{\partial q} = \frac{6.04\times 10^5 P}{T^2}

Observe that:

\frac{\partial N}{\partial T} = -\frac{P}{T}\cdot \frac{\partial N}{\partial P} - \frac{q}{T}\cdot \frac{\partial N}{\partial q}

so the three partial derivatives are linearly dependent. The Jacobian is a 1 \times 3 matrix (one observation), so its rank is at most 1. It is exactly 1 wherever at least one partial derivative is nonzero. \square

Corollary C.1.1. The null space of \partial N / \partial X is two-dimensional. The set of states \{X : N(X) = N_0\} is a two-dimensional manifold in (P,T,q)-space, and observations of N alone cannot distinguish points on this manifold.

Corollary C.1.2 (Null-space basis). A basis for the null space is:

v_1 = \begin{pmatrix} T/P \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad v_2 = \begin{pmatrix} -q/P \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}

Any perturbation of the form \delta X = a v_1 + b v_2 leaves N unchanged to first order.

C.3 Sensor Combination Algebra

Let H: \mathbb{R}^{n_X} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_Y} be the concatenated forward operator of a sensor suite. The state is locally identifiable at X_0 if \mathrm{rank}(\partial H / \partial X) = n_X.

Theorem C.2 (Identifiability of N + MWR + surface P). Consider the sensor suite:

· Y_N = N(X): radar refractivity (1 scalar).
· Y_{\mathrm{MWR}} = H_{\mathrm{MWR}}(T, q): microwave radiometer (multi-channel).
· Y_{P_s} = P(z=0): surface pressure (1 scalar).

If H_{\mathrm{MWR}} provides at least two independent constraints on T and q, then the concatenated Jacobian has rank 3 (full rank in the thermodynamic subspace).

Proof. The concatenated Jacobian is:

\frac{\partial H}{\partial (P,T,q)} = \begin{pmatrix} \partial N/\partial P & \partial N/\partial T & \partial N/\partial q \\ 0 & \partial H_{\mathrm{MWR}}/\partial T & \partial H_{\mathrm{MWR}}/\partial q \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}

The surface pressure row gives rank 1 in the P direction. The MWR submatrix, if rank 2, gives rank 2 in the (T,q) directions. The refractivity row adds one more independent constraint. Total rank = 3 if the refractivity row is not in the span of the other two. Since \partial N / \partial P \neq 0 and the surface row is orthogonal to T,q directions, the refractivity row is linearly independent. \square

Remark C.1. The v0.3 identifiability table claimed that \theta_v + surface P separates T,q. This is incorrect: one \theta_v observation plus one surface pressure gives two constraints for three unknowns (or three constraints including the surface pressure, but P is fixed). The corrected combinations require an additional humidity or temperature constraint—see Section 6.1 of the main text.

Theorem C.3 (Necessary condition for identifiability). A necessary condition for local identifiability of the full state X \in \mathbb{R}^{n_X} is:

\sum_{i=1}^{M} n_{Y_i} \geq n_X

Proof. The Jacobian \partial H / \partial X is an n_Y \times n_X matrix. Its rank is at most \min(n_Y, n_X). For the rank to equal n_X, we require n_Y \geq n_X. \square

Remark C.2. This condition is necessary but not sufficient. Additional constraints (background prior, physical constraints C(X)=0) can supply missing information. The posterior precision matrix \mathcal{P} = B^{-1} + H^\top R^{-1} H is the correct object; it can be full rank even when H^\top R^{-1} H is rank-deficient, provided B^{-1} supplies the missing directions.

