Appendix D — Observability and Posterior Precision

D.1 Observability Gramian vs Posterior Precision

Definition D.1 (Data-only observability Gramian).

\mathcal{O} = \sum_{i=1}^{M} H_i^\top R_i^{-1} H_i

Definition D.2 (Posterior precision matrix).

\mathcal{P} = B^{-1} + \mathcal{O}

Proposition D.1. The eigenvalues of \mathcal{P} dominate the eigenvalues of \mathcal{O} in the Loewner ordering:

\mathcal{P} \succeq \mathcal{O}

Proof. B^{-1} is positive definite (as the inverse of a covariance matrix). Therefore \mathcal{P} - \mathcal{O} = B^{-1} \succ 0. \square

Corollary D.1.1. The data-only Gramian \mathcal{O} systematically overstates the set of unobservable directions. A direction that is unobservable from data may still be constrained by the prior.

Definition D.3 (Directional observability). For a unit vector v \in \mathbb{R}^{n_X}, the directional observability is:

\kappa(v) = \frac{v^\top \mathcal{P} v}{v^\top B^{-1} v} = 1 + \frac{v^\top \mathcal{O} v}{v^\top B^{-1} v}

If \kappa(v) \approx 1, the direction v is dominated by the prior. If \kappa(v) \gg 1, the direction is dominated by data.

D.2 Bayesian Cramér–Rao Bound

Theorem D.1 (Bayesian Cramér–Rao bound). For any estimator \hat X(Y) of X, the mean-squared error satisfies:

\mathbb{E}\big[ (\hat X - X)(\hat X - X)^\top \big] \succeq \mathcal{P}^{-1} = \big( B^{-1} + \mathcal{O} \big)^{-1}

Proof. This is the standard Bayesian Cramér–Rao bound (Van Trees, 1968). The Fisher information matrix for the joint (X, Y) is:

\mathcal{J} = \mathbb{E}\left[ -\frac{\partial^2}{\partial X^2} \log p(X, Y) \right] = B^{-1} + \mathbb{E}\big[ H^\top R^{-1} H \big]

where the expectation is over Y. For Gaussian models, \mathbb{E}[H^\top R^{-1} H] is deterministic and equals \mathcal{O}. The Bayesian CRB states that the MSE of any estimator is bounded below by \mathcal{J}^{-1} = \mathcal{P}^{-1}. \square

Corollary D.1. The posterior standard deviation in any direction v satisfies:

\sqrt{v^\top \mathrm{Cov}(\hat X - X) v} \geq \sqrt{v^\top \mathcal{P}^{-1} v}

This provides a quantitative lower bound on achievable accuracy for each state component, which can be compared directly to operational thresholds (Section 14 of the main text).

D.3 Empirical Observability Gramian for Nonlinear Systems

For nonlinear H_i, the linearized Gramian is only locally valid. The empirical observability Gramian is:

\mathcal{O}_{\mathrm{emp}} = \sum_{j=1}^{N_s} \sum_{i=1}^{M} \frac{(H_i(X + \delta X_j) - H_i(X))^\top (H_i(X + \delta X_j) - H_i(X))}{\|\delta X_j\|^2}

where \{\delta X_j\}_{j=1}^{N_s} is a set of perturbation directions spanning the state space. The eigenvalues of \mathcal{O}_{\mathrm{emp}} quantify the average information content over the perturbation ensemble.

Remark D.1. For PIEP Phase I, we recommend computing both \mathcal{P} at the MAP estimate and \mathcal{O}_{\mathrm{emp}} across an ensemble of nature-run states to assess robustness of identifiability across the state distribution.

