Appendix E — Latent-Space Formulation and Constraint Enforcement

E.1 Autoencoder Construction

Let \mathrm{Enc}: \mathbb{R}^{n_X} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_z} and \mathrm{Dec}: \mathbb{R}^{n_z} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_X} be an encoder-decoder pair trained to minimize reconstruction error:

\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{AE}}(\mathrm{Enc}, \mathrm{Dec}) = \mathbb{E}_{X \sim p_{\mathrm{data}}} \big[ \| X - \mathrm{Dec}(\mathrm{Enc}(X)) \|^2 \big]

with regularization to encourage smoothness and physical consistency.

E.2 Why Latent-Space Covariance Is Approximately Diagonal

Proposition E.1. Let X be a random field with covariance B. Let z = \mathrm{Enc}(X) with Jacobian J_{\mathrm{Enc}}(X) = \partial z / \partial X. Under a first-order expansion around \bar X:

B_z = \mathrm{Cov}(z) \approx J_{\mathrm{Enc}}(\bar X) B J_{\mathrm{Enc}}(\bar X)^\top

If the encoder is trained so that the latent dimensions are disentangled—i.e., each latent dimension captures an independent physical mode—then B_z is approximately diagonal.

Proof. The autoencoder training objective implicitly encourages disentanglement through reconstruction pressure: if two latent dimensions were strongly correlated, a smaller latent dimension would suffice to reconstruct the same data, and the optimizer would tend to compress them. This is a heuristic, not a theorem. The precise statement depends on the architecture and regularization. \square

Remark E.1. This is the weakest theoretical point in the PIEP architecture. The claim that B_z is "nearly diagonal" is not generally guaranteed. It must be verified empirically for each planetary regime by computing the off-diagonal covariance of z on a held-out validation set. If the off-diagonal terms are non-negligible, the latent-space cost function must include the full B_z^{-1}, or the encoder must be retrained with an explicit decorrelation penalty.

E.3 Constraint Enforcement in Latent Space

Recall the state-space constraints:

C(X) = 0 \quad \text{(equality)}, \qquad G(X) \leq 0 \quad \text{(inequality)}

Three enforcement mechanisms are possible.

E.3.1 Architectural Enforcement

The decoder is constructed so that its output automatically satisfies C. For example:

· Mass conservation: if X includes a density field \rho, the decoder can be designed to output a divergence-free mass flux.
· Energy conservation: if X includes temperature and velocity, the decoder can enforce the internal energy budget by construction.
· Hydrostatic balance: for atmospheric states, the decoder can enforce \partial P / \partial z = -\rho g.

Formally, the decoder \mathrm{Dec} is constrained to lie in the feasible set:

\mathrm{Dec}: \mathbb{R}^{n_z} \to \{X : C(X) = 0\}

Lemma E.1. If \mathrm{Dec} maps into the feasible set, then any latent state z produces a physically consistent X = \mathrm{Dec}(z), and the equality constraint C(X)=0 is satisfied by construction.

Proof. Immediate from the definition of the decoder's range. \square

E.3.2 Penalty Enforcement

For constraints that cannot be enforced architecturally (typically inequality constraints), a penalty is added:

J_{\mathrm{penalty}}(z) = \mu_C \| C(\mathrm{Dec}(z)) \|^2 + \mu_G \sum_{k} \max(0, G_k(\mathrm{Dec}(z)))^2

where \mu_C, \mu_G > 0 are penalty weights. The modified cost is:

J_{\mathrm{total}}(z, \theta) = J(z, \theta) + J_{\mathrm{penalty}}(z)

Proposition E.2 (Penalty convergence). As \mu_C, \mu_G \to \infty, the minimizer of J_{\mathrm{total}} converges to the constrained minimizer of J over \Omega.

Proof. This is the standard penalty method for constrained optimization. For each \mu, the minimizer z^*_\mu satisfies C(\mathrm{Dec}(z^*_\mu)) \to 0 and G_k(\mathrm{Dec}(z^*_\mu))_+ \to 0 as \mu \to \infty, under mild regularity conditions. \square

E.3.3 Projection Enforcement

After each optimization step, the decoded state is projected onto \Omega:

X^{(k+1)} = \Pi_\Omega \big( \mathrm{Dec}(z^{(k+1)}) \big)

where \Pi_\Omega is the Euclidean projection onto the feasible set. The latent state is then re-encoded:

z^{(k+1)} \leftarrow \mathrm{Enc}(X^{(k+1)})

Remark E.2. Projection is exact but expensive. It is used only for constraints where architectural and penalty methods are insufficient—typically for global conservation laws that must be satisfied to machine precision.

E.4 Quantifying Constraint Violation

The constraint residual is:

r_C(z) = \| C(\mathrm{Dec}(z)) \|, \qquad r_G(z) = \| \max(0, G(\mathrm{Dec}(z))) \|

These are reported as falsifiability metrics (Section 14 of the main text). If r_C(z) is not significantly smaller than the constraint residual of an unconstrained inversion, the latent-space constraint enforcement has failed.

