Appendix F — Forward Model Operators

F.1 Radar Refractivity Operator

The refractivity along a ray path \Gamma is:

N(\mathbf{x}) = 77.6 \frac{P(\mathbf{x})}{T(\mathbf{x})} + 6.04\times 10^5 \frac{P(\mathbf{x}) q(\mathbf{x})}{T(\mathbf{x})^2}

The phase delay along the ray is:

\Delta \phi = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \int_\Gamma N(\mathbf{x})\, d\ell

where \lambda is the radar wavelength and d\ell is the path element. The observation operator is:

H_{\mathrm{radar}}(X, \theta) = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \int_\Gamma N(X(\mathbf{x}))\, d\ell + \theta_{\mathrm{phase}}

where \theta_{\mathrm{phase}} is a calibration phase offset.

Identifiability limitation. As shown in Theorem C.1, N has a two-dimensional null space in (P,T,q). The refractivity operator cannot separate pressure, temperature, and humidity without additional constraints.

F.2 Microwave Radiometer Operator

The microwave brightness temperature at frequency \nu and viewing angle \mu = \cos\theta is:

T_B(\nu, \mu) = \int_0^\infty T(z)\, W_\nu(z, \mu)\, dz + (1 - \epsilon_s) T_{\mathrm{sky}}(\nu, \mu) e^{-\tau_\nu}

where:

· W_\nu(z, \mu) is the weighting function, W_\nu = \frac{\partial \tau_\nu}{\partial z} e^{-\tau_\nu(z)}.
· \tau_\nu(z) = \int_z^\infty \kappa_\nu(z')\, dz' is the optical depth.
· \kappa_\nu is the absorption coefficient, depending on P, T, q, and cloud liquid water.
· \epsilon_s is the surface emissivity.
· T_{\mathrm{sky}} is the cosmic background.

The observation operator is:

H_{\mathrm{MW}}(X, \theta) = T_B(\nu, \mu; X) + \theta_{\mathrm{MW}}

with \theta_{\mathrm{MW}} capturing calibration offsets and channel-dependent biases.

F.3 Doppler Lidar Operator

The Doppler lidar measures the line-of-sight velocity v_{\mathrm{LOS}} and the backscattered intensity. The velocity is:

v_{\mathrm{LOS}} = \mathbf{u} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}

where \hat{\mathbf{n}} is the line-of-sight unit vector.

The TKE-budget buoyancy residual retrieval of \theta_v follows Xian et al. (2026). The TKE budget is:

\frac{\partial e}{\partial t} + \mathbf{u} \cdot \nabla e = - \overline{u'w'} \frac{\partial U}{\partial z} - \overline{v'w'} \frac{\partial V}{\partial z} + \frac{g}{\theta_v} \overline{w'\theta_v'} - \epsilon

where e is TKE, \epsilon is dissipation, and \overline{w'\theta_v'} is the buoyancy flux. Solving for \theta_v from the residual:

\theta_v = \frac{g\, \overline{w'\theta_v'}}{\frac{\partial e}{\partial t} + \mathbf{u}\cdot\nabla e + \overline{u'w'} \frac{\partial U}{\partial z} + \overline{v'w'} \frac{\partial V}{\partial z} + \epsilon}

The observation operator is:

H_{\mathrm{lidar}}(X, \theta) = \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{u} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}} \\ \theta_v(X) \end{pmatrix} + \theta_{\mathrm{lidar}}

F.4 GNSS Radio Occultation Operator

The bending angle \alpha is related to the refractivity profile by the Abel transform:

\alpha(a) = -2a \int_a^\infty \frac{d\ln n / dr}{\sqrt{r^2 - a^2}}\, dr

where n = 1 + N \times 10^{-6} is the refractive index, r is the radius, and a = r n is the impact parameter. The observation operator is:

H_{\mathrm{RO}}(X, \theta) = \alpha(a; X) + \theta_{\mathrm{RO}}

F.5 Surface Station Operator

Surface stations (e.g., MEDA) observe directly:

H_{\mathrm{surf}}(X, \theta) = \begin{pmatrix} P(z=0) \\ T(z=0) \\ q(z=0) \\ \mathbf{u}(z=0) \\ D(z=0) \\ R(z=0) \end{pmatrix} + \theta_{\mathrm{surf}}

F.6 Orbital Sounding Operator

Orbital sounders (e.g., MCS, Diviner) observe radiances that are inverted to profiles. The forward model is a radiative transfer calculation similar to H_{\mathrm{MW}} or H_{\mathrm{IR}}, but with a limb-sounding geometry:

H_{\mathrm{orb}}(X, \theta) = \int T(z) W_\nu^{\mathrm{limb}}(z) \, dz + \theta_{\mathrm{orb}}

