Appendix G — Uncertainty Calibration Theory

G.1 Continuous Ranked Probability Score

Definition G.1 (CRPS). For a predictive distribution F and observation y:

\mathrm{CRPS}(F, y) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \big[ F(x) - \mathbb{1}\{x \geq y\} \big]^2 \, dx

For a Gaussian predictive distribution \mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma^2):

\mathrm{CRPS}(\mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma^2), y) = \sigma \left[ \frac{y - \mu}{\sigma} \left( 2\Phi\left(\frac{y-\mu}{\sigma}\right) - 1 \right) + 2\phi\left(\frac{y-\mu}{\sigma}\right) - \frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}} \right]

where \Phi and \phi are the standard normal CDF and PDF.

Proposition G.1 (Properness). CRPS is a proper scoring rule: it is minimized in expectation when F equals the true data-generating distribution.

Proof. Standard result for proper scoring rules; see Gneiting & Raftery (2007). \square

G.2 Probability Integral Transform

Definition G.2 (PIT). For a predictive CDF F and observation y, the PIT value is:

u = F(y)

If F is correctly calibrated, u \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(0,1).

Proposition G.2 (Calibration diagnostic). A histogram of PIT values that is not uniform indicates miscalibration:

· U-shaped histogram: overconfident (predictive intervals too narrow).
· Bell-shaped histogram: underconfident (predictive intervals too wide).
· Skewed histogram: biased predictions.

Definition G.3 (KS statistic). The Kolmogorov–Smirnov statistic for calibration is:

D_{\mathrm{KS}} = \sup_{u \in [0,1]} | \hat F_{\mathrm{PIT}}(u) - u |

where \hat F_{\mathrm{PIT}} is the empirical CDF of PIT values. The threshold D_{\mathrm{KS}} > 0.15 (main text Section 14) corresponds approximately to a 5% rejection level for N \approx 100 samples.

G.3 Spread-Skill Ratio

Definition G.4 (Spread-skill ratio). For an ensemble of predictions with spread \sigma_{\mathrm{spread}} and errors e_i = \hat X_i - X_i^{\mathrm{true}}:

\mathrm{SSR} = \frac{\sigma_{\mathrm{spread}}}{\sqrt{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} e_i^2}}

If SSR > 1, the ensemble is over-dispersive (underconfident). If SSR < 1, the ensemble is under-dispersive (overconfident). The target interval [0.8, 1.25] (main text Section 14) is a common operational standard.

G.4 Out-of-Distribution Detection

Definition G.5 (Latent Mahalanobis distance). For a latent state z and training latent distribution \mathcal{N}(z_b, B_z):

d_M(z) = \sqrt{(z - z_b)^\top B_z^{-1} (z - z_b)}

Definition G.6 (OOD flag). The state is flagged OOD if:

d_M(z) > \chi^2_{n_z, 1-\alpha}

where \chi^2_{n_z, 1-\alpha} is the (1-\alpha)-quantile of the chi-squared distribution with n_z degrees of freedom.

Proposition G.3 (Reconstruction error as OOD indicator). If the autoencoder is trained on p_{\mathrm{data}}, then the reconstruction error:

r_{\mathrm{rec}}(X) = \| X - \mathrm{Dec}(\mathrm{Enc}(X)) \|

is small for in-distribution X and large for OOD X.

Proof. This is a heuristic, not a theorem. In practice, reconstruction error is a reliable OOD indicator when the encoder is regularized to produce a smooth latent space. Deep autoencoders can sometimes reconstruct OOD inputs well, so PIEP uses both d_M(z) and r_{\mathrm{rec}}(X) as OOD signals. \square

