Appendix H — Decision-Theoretic Formalism

H.1 Conditional Value-at-Risk

Definition H.1 (Value-at-Risk). For a loss L with CDF F_L, the value-at-risk at confidence level \alpha \in (0,1) is:

\mathrm{VaR}_\alpha(L) = \inf\{ \ell : F_L(\ell) \geq \alpha \}

Definition H.2 (Conditional Value-at-Risk). The conditional value-at-risk is:

\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L) = \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \int_\alpha^1 \mathrm{VaR}_\tau(L) \, d\tau

Equivalently, for continuous distributions:

\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L) = \mathbb{E}[L \mid L \geq \mathrm{VaR}_\alpha(L)]

H.2 Coherence Properties

Theorem H.1 (CVaR is a coherent risk measure). CVaR satisfies:

1. Monotonicity: L_1 \leq L_2 \Rightarrow \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L_1) \leq \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L_2).
2. Positive homogeneity: \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(\lambda L) = \lambda \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L) for \lambda > 0.
3. Translation invariance: \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L + c) = \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L) + c.
4. Subadditivity: \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L_1 + L_2) \leq \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L_1) + \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L_2).

Proof. These are the standard coherence axioms of Artzner et al. (1999). CVaR satisfies all four; VaR satisfies (1), (2), (3) but not (4). \square

Remark H.1. Subadditivity is critical for the PIEP decision interface: it ensures that diversifying across multiple risky actions does not increase aggregate risk. VaR fails this property, which is why PIEP uses CVaR.

H.3 Risk-Averse Decision Policy

Definition H.3 (Optimal CVaR policy). Given the posterior p(X \mid Y) and a loss function L(a, X):

\pi^*(a \mid \hat X, U_X) = \arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \; \mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha \big[ L(a, X) \mid Y \big]

Theorem H.2 (Rockafellar–Uryasev representation). The CVaR minimization can be reformulated as:

\mathrm{CVaR}_\alpha(L) = \min_{t \in \mathbb{R}} \left[ t + \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \mathbb{E}\big[ (L - t)_+ \big] \right]

where (L-t)_+ = \max(0, L-t).

Proof. See Rockafellar & Uryasev (2000). \square

Corollary H.2.1 (Computational tractability). For a finite sample \{X^{(j)}\}_{j=1}^{N} from the posterior, the CVaR can be estimated by solving a linear program:

\min_{t, u_j} \; t + \frac{1}{N(1-\alpha)} \sum_{j=1}^{N} u_j \quad \text{s.t.} \quad u_j \geq L(a, X^{(j)}) - t, \; u_j \geq 0

This makes CVaR-based decision-making tractable even with high-dimensional state spaces.

H.4 Application: Rover Traverse Planning

For a rover planning a traverse a = \{(x_k, y_k)\}_{k=1}^{K}, the loss function is:

L(a, X) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \big[ \lambda_T L_T(x_k, T) + \lambda_P L_P(x_k, D) + \lambda_S L_S(x_k, S) \big]

where L_T penalizes thermal exposure, L_P penalizes power loss from dust, and L_S penalizes slip risk. The optimal traverse is:

a^* = \arg\min_a \; \mathrm{CVaR}_{0.95}\big[ L(a, X) \mid \hat X, U_X \big]

