Appendix M — Open Theoretical Problems

The following theoretical questions remain open and are flagged for future work:

1. Latent-space covariance diagonalization. Under what precise conditions on the autoencoder architecture and training data is B_z approximately diagonal? A rigorous theorem would require a formal definition of disentanglement and a proof that the autoencoder training objective induces it.
2. Nonlinear identifiability. The rank analysis in Appendix C is local (first-order). A global identifiability analysis for the full multimodal observation system requires algebraic geometry tools (e.g., differential algebra, characteristic sets) that have not yet been applied to this problem.
3. Cross-sensor representativeness covariance. The full covariance R including cross-sensor terms (Appendix A.4) is generally not known. Estimating it from nature-run data introduces its own sampling error, which must be propagated into the posterior precision.
4. CVaR with model uncertainty. The CVaR policy in Appendix H assumes the posterior p(X \mid Y) is correctly calibrated. If the posterior is miscalibrated (Layer 4.5 failure), the CVaR estimate is biased. A robust CVaR formulation that accounts for calibration uncertainty is an open problem.
5. Planetary transfer metrics. The 60% forward-operator reuse threshold (Criterion 5) is a heuristic. A rigorous theory of physical model transferability across planetary regimes would require a formal definition of "physics-preserving" model transformation.
6. Computational scaling. The latent-space inversion has complexity O(n_z^3) for the linear solve. For n_z \sim 10^3, this is tractable. For n_z \sim 10^5 (global km-scale Earth), sparse or low-rank approximations are required. The trade-off between accuracy and computational cost is not yet characterized.

